cademy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5573.json" "file_sha256" "ecb104155aeae61a2ab41c4c0d3e119b278e0e484a85827f7a85642a79a34435" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5573.json" "file_sha256" "ecb104155aeae61a2ab41c4c0d3e119b278e0e484a85827f7a85642a79a34435" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7173.json" "file_sha256" "a0e6a36f3154b2a41f97bf583ff4c86808b27498e9f46a8823a9137f019d8975" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by   \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{13\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{13\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7173.json" "file_sha256" "a0e6a36f3154b2a41f97bf583ff4c86808b27498e9f46a8823a9137f019d8975" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5574.json" "file_sha256" "354577d0d954af00ccf824bd3ff9351a93771a41b5d4a8130ab20917bfa49a7a" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-2)}^{2}+{(2-2)}^{2}+{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5574.json" "file_sha256" "354577d0d954af00ccf824bd3ff9351a93771a41b5d4a8130ab20917bfa49a7a" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7309.json" "file_sha256" "1e3776107486f2968a71731a723aa2760c220042c1d8b831b95deb7b7608e267" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{10})}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\,\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10\\,\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7309.json" "file_sha256" "1e3776107486f2968a71731a723aa2760c220042c1d8b831b95deb7b7608e267" "basis" 1463 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-40218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+9\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "748144e91d47d8b68a80cf7b0ffb60680993bc9ca26aa4558cf96603d9db5041"} {"question" "q-40218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-81475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-81475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{24}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{35}{35}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{4\\cdot 11+6\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 0+(-17)\\cdot (-8)}{{11}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-8)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{210}{210}[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-81475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/questio