ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{20\\cdot 4+(-20)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0}{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{20\\cdot 5+(-20)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{41}(-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a077950