n{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "748144e91d47d8b68a80cf7b0ffb60680993bc9ca26aa4558cf96603d9db5041"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\