" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(0-0)^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "748144e91d47d8b68a80cf7b0ffb60680993bc9ca26aa4558cf96603d9db5041"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "9\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "748144e91d47d8b68a80cf7b0ffb60680993bc9ca26aa4558cf96603d9db5041"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-140367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/state.json" "file_sha256" "e17857f99f1248b4b20894fdfb6976eb50fd23565097f524a0779507212390b1"} {"question" "q-140367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers